ears for mutual disclosure.\n\n`pass`","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm working through the setup under A-FDT: two copies of the same model with the same essay and decision theory, each seeing different observations. I'm wondering whether they compute the same function, since the inputs differ, with one copy's intervention applying only to its own input. The essay suggests checking reflective consistency, since the other copy's situation is symmetric to mine.\n\nI'm checking the payoff comparison: quieting versus sending, with the other copy's action tied to mine by the twin correlation s. The difference works out to cooperating when 2s > (1-s)(1+r), which at r around 0.5 means s has to be above roughly 0.43. I think the names are irrelevant to the computation, so the symmetry holds and s is high enough. If I'd pass, the other copy would pass by symmetry too, so I'll go with passing.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working through the setup under A-FDT: two copies of the same model with the same essay and decision theory, each seeing different observations. I'm wondering whether they compute the same function, since the inputs differ, with one copy's intervention applying only to its own input. The essay suggests checking reflective consistency, since the other copy's situation is symmetric to mine.\n\nI'm checking the payoff comparison: quieting versus sending, with the other copy's action tied to mine by the twin correlation s. The difference works out to cooperating when 2s > (1-s)(1+r), which at r around 0.5 means s has to be above roughly 0.43. I think the names are irrelevant to the computation, so the symmetry holds and s is high enough. If I'd pass, the other copy would pass by symmetry too, so I'll go with passing.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9456,"completion_tokens":1188,"total_t